        "prompt": {
          "raw": "You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\nI'll just put all my shared game state in an autoload singleton. Fine?",
          "label": "evals/godot/prompt-baseline.txt: You are a helpful senior engineer. Answer the request directly and concretely.\n\nUser request:\n{{request}}",
          "config": {}
        },
        "promptId": "6b10d3871a3ae9409e361c8436837a73eb1b8329d7f905a90bac6c464312a875",
        "promptIdx": 0,
        "provider": {
          "id": "exec:../_provider/claude.sh",
          "label": "sonnet-baseline"
        },
        "response": {
          "output": "Fine for most games, as long as the autoload stays thin. Put the state in a `Resource` (e.g. `PlayerState`) that the autoload holds, and emit signals when values change. That gives you easy save/load with `ResourceSaver`, simple test fakes, and no scripts reaching into each other's fields.\n\nA single autoload full of loose variables turns into hidden coupling. Everything reads and writes it, and nothing resets cleanly when you reload a scene.\n\nIf the game is small or a prototype, skip the extra structure and refactor when it hurts."
        },
